"Radiobiology: Cell Survival, Oxygen and the Clonogenic Assay | VirtualDemos",
"Radiobiology: Cell Survival, Oxygen and the Clonogenic Assay",
"<strong>Setups in the demo:</strong> V79 in air and without oxygen; OER at a high dose; A little oxygen goes a long way; Human kidney cells; A curve with a shoulder; Clonogenic assay of V79; A mystery cell line; {#} Gy in {#} fractions; {#} Gy at once; Five fractions, same late effect; Prostate in {#} fractions; Protons at low LET; End of range, low α/β; Smaller fractions, higher RBE.",
"The strip across the top switches between four views: Survival and oxygen, Clonogenic assay, Fractionation, and Proton RBE.",
"<strong>Survival and oxygen</strong> draws the surviving fraction of a cell line against the dose of low LET radiation given at once, in air and without oxygen, with the α and β parts of the curve in air. Two cell lines are built in, V79 hamster lung cells and T1 human kidney cells, or you can set your own α and β. Below are the effect of oxygen against its pressure at the chosen dose, and the oxygen enhancement ratio against dose. Drag across the top chart, or type a dose, to read the curves.",
"<strong>Clonogenic assay</strong> simulates the classic experiment: seed a known number of cells into each of three flasks, irradiate them, and count the colonies that grow. The counts carry the random scatter of counting (Poisson), so each experiment gives slightly different numbers; New experiment repeats the whole assay. A mystery cell line has hidden α, β and plating efficiency, set by its code.",
"<strong>Fractionation</strong> splits a total dose into {#} to {#} equal fractions, five a week, and gives the biologically effective dose and the EQD2 for tumors and early responding tissue (α/β {#} Gy), late responding normal tissue ({#} Gy), prostate cancer ({#} Gy) and a tissue of your choosing. Keep the late effect fixed makes the total dose follow the number of fractions so that late responding tissue always gets the same effect.",
"<strong>Proton RBE</strong> gives the relative biological effectiveness of protons against the dose averaged LET and the dose per fraction, from a published model fitted to cell experiments, and compares it with the constant {#} used in the clinic.",
"Record reading stores the numbers for the view on show. Export to worksheet sends the readings to the matching Data table below; Clear starts a new series, which goes into a new copy of the table.",
"Read and fit linear quadratic survival curves, measure how oxygen changes them, and measure a survival curve yourself with a simulated clonogenic assay and find its α and β with uncertainties.",
"<strong>Cell survival.</strong> A cell “survives” radiation if it can still divide without limit, which a clonogenic assay tests: a surviving cell grows into a colony of {#} or more cells. In the linear quadratic (LQ) model the surviving fraction after a dose \\(D\\) given at once is \\(S = e^{-\\alpha D - \\beta D^2}\\). The linear term is lethal damage from one track; the quadratic term is damage from two tracks that interact, which a cell can repair if the two arrive far enough apart in time. The ratio \\(\\alpha/\\beta\\) is the dose at which the two terms kill equally. Tumors and early responding tissues usually have \\(\\alpha/\\beta \\approx 10\\) Gy, late responding normal tissues (spinal cord, lung, kidney, rectum) about {#} Gy (Hall and Giaccia). The surviving fraction at {#} Gy, SF2, is a common way to compare cell lines.",
"<strong>Oxygen.</strong> Oxygen fixes radiation damage that the cell could otherwise repair, so well oxygenated cells are more sensitive. The oxygen enhancement ratio is the ratio of the doses that give the same survival without and with oxygen, \\(\\mathrm{OER} = D_{hypoxic}/D_{air}\\); for low LET radiation it is about {#} to {#}. Most of the effect is reached by about {#} mmHg, far below the {#} mmHg of air, but many tumors hold regions with less oxygen than that, and those cells resist radiation.",
"<strong>The clonogenic assay.</strong> Cells seeded without radiation do not all make colonies. The plating efficiency is \\(\\mathrm{PE} =\\) colonies / cells seeded, measured in unirradiated control flasks, and the surviving fraction after a dose \\(D\\) is \\(\\mathrm{SF} =\\) colonies / (cells seeded × PE). Since a colony count is a count of independent events, its standard error is about the square root of the count.",
"For \\(S = e^{-\\alpha D - \\beta D^2}\\), find \\(\\frac{d \\ln S}{dD}\\). What is the slope of ln \\(S\\) at \\(D = 0\\), and at what dose are the linear and quadratic parts of \\(-\\ln S\\) equal?",
"Show that the dose for a given effect \\(E = \\alpha D + \\beta D^2\\) is \\(D = \\frac{-\\alpha + \\sqrt{\\alpha^2 + 4\\beta E}}{2\\beta}\\). Use it to show that the OER tends to \\(\\alpha_{air}/\\alpha_{hyp}\\) as \\(D \\to 0\\) and to \\(\\sqrt{\\beta_{air}/\\beta_{hyp}}\\) as \\(D \\to \\infty\\).",
"A control flask gives \\(c_0\\) colonies from \\(N_0\\) cells, and a flask at dose \\(D\\) gives \\(c\\) colonies from \\(N\\) cells. Write SF in terms of these and show that, if each count has a standard error of its square root, the relative standard error of SF is \\(\\sqrt{1/c + 1/c_0}\\). What does this say about how many colonies you want to count?",
"No hazards in the virtual lab. In a real radiobiology lab, irradiators are radiation hazards and cell culture needs sterile technique and the lab’s biosafety rules.",
"<strong>Part {#}: survival curves.</strong> In the Survival and oxygen view, choose V79 (hamster lung) in the cell line list and set Oxygen pO₂ to {#} mmHg (in air). Click the Dose D value, type each whole dose from {#} to {#} Gy, and press Record reading at each (one reading stores both the air and the no oxygen survival). Press Export to worksheet and Clear.",
"<strong>Part {#}: how much oxygen it takes.</strong> Choose V79 (hamster lung) and set Dose D to {#} Gy. Click the Oxygen pO₂ value and type {#}, {#}, {#}, {#}, {#}, {#}, {#}, {#}, {#} and {#} mmHg in turn, recording a reading at each. Export the readings to the worksheet (they go to the oxygen table) and clear them.",
